---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Get Started - AI Training Guide">
  <PageShell chapterId="practical-exercise" subChapterId="get-started">
    <div class="prose space-y-6">
      <p>
        This walkthrough gets your machine ready for the practical exercises. Work through each step in order.
        If you already have a tool installed, verify it works and move on.
      </p>

      <p>
        By the end of this page you'll have the sample project running locally, pushed to your own GitHub repo,
        and an AI coding tool connected and ready to go.
      </p>

      <!-- ─── Step 1 ─── -->
      <div class="relative border-l-4 border-teal-400 pl-6 py-2 my-10">
        <div class="absolute -left-4 top-2 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-teal-500 text-white text-sm font-bold flex items-center justify-center">1</div>
        <h3 class="mt-0 mb-2">Prerequisites</h3>
        <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm mb-4">
          Install the tools you'll need for the exercises. If you already have Homebrew, Node.js, pnpm, gh, and git
          installed, skip ahead to Step 2.
        </p>

        <p class="text-sm text-stone-700 mb-2 font-medium">Install Homebrew (macOS package manager):</p>
        <div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-4 font-mono text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-wrap">{`/bin/bash -c "$(curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Homebrew/install/HEAD/install.sh)"`}</div>

        <Callout variant="tip" title="Troubleshooting">
          <p>
            If <code>brew</code> isn't recognized after installing, close and reopen your terminal. On Apple Silicon
            Macs you may also need to run the shell configuration command that the Homebrew installer prints at the end.
          </p>
        </Callout>

        <p class="text-sm text-stone-700 mb-2 font-medium">Install the required tools via Homebrew:</p>
        <div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-4 font-mono text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-wrap">{`brew install fnm pnpm gh git`}</div>

        <p class="text-sm text-stone-700 mb-2 font-medium">Install and activate Node.js 22:</p>
        <div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-4 font-mono text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-wrap">{`fnm install 22
fnm use 22`}</div>

        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600">
          Verify everything is working by running <code>node -v</code>, <code>pnpm -v</code>, and <code>gh --version</code>.
          All three should print a version number.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- ─── Step 2 ─── -->
      <div class="relative border-l-4 border-teal-400 pl-6 py-2 my-10">
        <div class="absolute -left-4 top-2 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-teal-500 text-white text-sm font-bold flex items-center justify-center">2</div>
        <h3 class="mt-0 mb-2">Check Repository Access</h3>
        <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm mb-4">
          Before cloning anything, confirm you can see the training repository on GitHub.
        </p>

        <p class="text-sm text-stone-700 mb-2">
          Open <a href="https://github.com/konradgroup/ai-training-2026" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-teal-600 underline hover:text-teal-800">github.com/konradgroup/ai-training-2026</a> in your browser.
        </p>

        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 mb-2">
          You should see the repository's file listing. If you get a 404 or "Not Found" page, you don't have access yet.
        </p>

        <Callout variant="warning" title="Can't see the repo?">
          <p>
            Contact your manager to request access to the <strong>konradgroup/ai-training-2026</strong> repository.
            You'll need at least read access to continue.
          </p>
        </Callout>

        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600">
          Don't clone the full repo. The next step extracts just the sample project folder you need.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- ─── Step 3 ─── -->
      <div class="relative border-l-4 border-teal-400 pl-6 py-2 my-10">
        <div class="absolute -left-4 top-2 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-teal-500 text-white text-sm font-bold flex items-center justify-center">3</div>
        <h3 class="mt-0 mb-2">Log In to GitHub CLI</h3>
        <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm mb-4">
          You'll need to be authenticated with the GitHub CLI so you can access the private training repo
          and create your own repository later.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-4 font-mono text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-wrap">{`gh auth login`}</div>

        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600">
          Follow the prompts to log in with your GitHub account. If you're already logged in,
          you can verify with <code>gh auth status</code>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- ─── Step 4 ─── -->
      <div class="relative border-l-4 border-teal-400 pl-6 py-2 my-10">
        <div class="absolute -left-4 top-2 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-teal-500 text-white text-sm font-bold flex items-center justify-center">4</div>
        <h3 class="mt-0 mb-2">Extract the Sample Project</h3>
        <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm mb-4">
          The training repo contains several folders, but you only need the <code>sample-project</code> directory.
          This command downloads the <code>sample-project</code> into a new <code>ai-training-exercise</code> directory.
        </p>

        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 mb-2">
          Run the following from wherever you keep your projects (e.g. <code>~/projects</code>):
        </p>

        <div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-4 font-mono text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-wrap">{`GITHUB_TOKEN=$(gh auth token) pnpx degit konradgroup/ai-training-2026/sample-project ai-training-exercise`}</div>

        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 mb-4">
          If that command fails, use this git alternative instead:
        </p>

        <pre class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-4 font-mono text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto"><code>{`git clone --no-checkout --depth 1 --filter=blob:none \\
          "https://oauth2:$(gh auth token)@github.com/konradgroup/ai-training-2026.git" \\
          ai-training-exercise \\
          && cd ai-training-exercise \\
          && git sparse-checkout set --no-cone sample-project/ \\
          && git checkout \\
          && mv sample-project/{.,}* . 2>/dev/null \\
          ; rm -rf sample-project .git`}</code></pre>

        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600">
          You should now have a clean project folder called <code>ai-training-exercise</code> with no <code>.git</code> directory inside it.
          If you run <code>ls ai-training-exercise</code>, you should see files like <code>package.json</code>, <code>tsconfig.json</code>, and a <code>src/</code> folder.
        </p>

        <Callout variant="warning" title="SSH key authorization required">
          <p>
            If you encounter an error running either command, make sure you have an SSH key associated with your GitHub account
            that is authorized for use within the konradgroup organization.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-2">
            <a href="https://github.com/settings/keys" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-teal-600 underline hover:text-teal-800">You can check your SSH keys here.</a>{" "}
            Next to the relevant key, click <strong>Configure SSO</strong> and ensure that <strong>konradgroup</strong>{" "}
            appears under the "Available to authorize" section. If it does, ensure it is authorized before continuing.
          </p>
        </Callout>
      </div>

      <!-- ─── Step 5 ─── -->
      <div class="relative border-l-4 border-teal-400 pl-6 py-2 my-10">
        <div class="absolute -left-4 top-2 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-teal-500 text-white text-sm font-bold flex items-center justify-center">5</div>
        <h3 class="mt-0 mb-2">Install Dependencies</h3>
        <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm mb-4">
          From the <code>ai-training-exercise</code> folder, install the project's npm packages.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-4 font-mono text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-wrap">{`pnpm install`}</div>

        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600">
          This should take less than a minute. If you see peer dependency warnings, that's normal and safe to ignore.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- ─── Step 6 ─── -->
      <div class="relative border-l-4 border-teal-400 pl-6 py-2 my-10">
        <div class="absolute -left-4 top-2 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-teal-500 text-white text-sm font-bold flex items-center justify-center">6</div>
        <h3 class="mt-0 mb-2">Initialize Git and Push to GitHub</h3>
        <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm mb-4">
          Create your own repository so you can track progress, practice branching, and experiment freely
          without affecting the shared training repo.
        </p>

        <p class="text-sm text-stone-700 mb-2 font-medium">Initialize a fresh Git repo and make your first commit:</p>
        <div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-4 font-mono text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-wrap">{`git init
git add .
git commit -m "Initial commit: sample project setup"`}</div>

        <p class="text-sm text-stone-700 mb-2 font-medium">Create a private GitHub repo and push:</p>
        <div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-4 font-mono text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-wrap">{`gh repo create ai-training-exercise --private --source=. --push`}</div>

        <Callout variant="info" title="Why your own repo?">
          <p>
            Having your own repo lets you commit freely, try things, make mistakes, and use Git to undo them.
            Several exercises involve branching and pull request workflows, so you'll need push access to a repo you own.
          </p>
        </Callout>
      </div>

      <!-- ─── Step 7 ─── -->
      <div class="relative border-l-4 border-teal-400 pl-6 py-2 my-10">
        <div class="absolute -left-4 top-2 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-teal-500 text-white text-sm font-bold flex items-center justify-center">7</div>
        <h3 class="mt-0 mb-2">Run the App</h3>
        <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm mb-4">
          Start the development server and make sure everything works.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-4 font-mono text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-wrap">{`pnpm dev`}</div>

        <p class="text-sm text-stone-700 mb-3">
          Open <a href="http://localhost:5173" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-teal-600 underline hover:text-teal-800">http://localhost:5173</a> in your browser. You should see the dashboard app running.
        </p>

        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 mb-2">
          Take a minute to click around and explore what's already built. You'll notice that some features are fully
          implemented while others are stubs or have intentional bugs. That's by design. The exercises will have you
          build out missing features and fix issues using AI tools.
        </p>

        <Callout variant="success" title="Checkpoint">
          <p>
            If you can see the dashboard in your browser, your project setup is working. Leave the dev server running
            in this terminal and open a new terminal tab for the next step.
          </p>
        </Callout>
      </div>

      <!-- ─── Step 8 ─── -->
      <div class="relative border-l-4 border-teal-400 pl-6 py-2 my-10">
        <div class="absolute -left-4 top-2 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-teal-500 text-white text-sm font-bold flex items-center justify-center">8</div>
        <h3 class="mt-0 mb-2">Set Up Your AI Tool</h3>
        <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm mb-4">
          The exercises use Claude Code as the primary example, though the concepts apply across AI coding tools
          (Cursor, Gemini CLI, Codex, etc.).
        </p>

        <Callout variant="info" title="Need a Claude Code account?">
          <p>
            Contact IT to get a Claude Code training account provisioned before continuing.
          </p>
        </Callout>

        <p class="text-sm text-stone-700 mb-2 font-medium">Install Claude Code (native installer for macOS / Linux):</p>
        <div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-4 font-mono text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-wrap">{`curl -fsSL https://claude.ai/install.sh | bash`}</div>

        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 mb-4">
          Alternatively, you can install it with Homebrew (<code>brew install --cask claude-code</code>) or npm (<code>npm install -g @anthropic-ai/claude-code</code>).
          If the <code>claude</code> command isn't recognized right away, close and reopen your terminal.
        </p>

        <p class="text-sm text-stone-700 mb-2 font-medium">Launch Claude Code and sign in:</p>
        <div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-4 font-mono text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed whitespace-pre-wrap">{`claude`}</div>

        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 mb-3">
          Running <code>claude</code> for the first time in your <code>ai-training-exercise</code> folder will open a browser window and walk you through authentication.
          Sign in with the Claude Code training account provided by IT. (You can also run <code>/login</code> inside an active session at any time to switch accounts.)
        </p>

        <Callout variant="tip" title="Using a different tool?">
          <p>
            If your team is using Cursor, Gemini CLI, or another AI tool, follow the setup instructions provided by
            your manager. The exercises will note where tool-specific commands differ.
          </p>
        </Callout>

        <p class="text-sm text-stone-700 mb-2 font-medium">Verify the tool can see your project:</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600">
          Open your AI tool in the <code>ai-training-exercise</code> directory and ask it something simple like
          "What files are in this project?" If it lists your project files, you're connected and ready.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- ─── Step 9 ─── -->
      <div class="relative border-l-4 border-teal-400 pl-6 py-2 my-10">
        <div class="absolute -left-4 top-2 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-teal-500 text-white text-sm font-bold flex items-center justify-center">9</div>
        <h3 class="mt-0 mb-2">You're Ready</h3>
        <p class="text-stone-600 text-sm mb-4">
          Your environment is set up: the sample project is running, your code is in a personal GitHub repo, and
          your AI tool is connected to the project.
        </p>

        <Callout variant="success" title="What's next">
          <p>
            The next page walks you through exploring the codebase and fixing your first bugs with AI. You'll start
            by reading the project structure, then move into hands-on exercises that build on each other.
          </p>
        </Callout>
      </div>
    </div>
  </PageShell>
</MainLayout>
